### API type
{% if apiType == 'graphql' %}
Google Cloud has no managed GraphQL service. The function is the GraphQL
server{% if gateway %} and the gateway publishes `POST /graphql` and `GET /graphql`{% else %}; Cloud Armor rejects every path except `/graphql` with 404{% endif %}.
The function decides what a `GET` request does (queries in the URL, or a
GraphQL IDE). The schema lives with the function code, not in this repository.
{%- else %}
API Gateway is one product; there is no separate REST and HTTP flavour on
Google Cloud, so `rest` and `http` create the same resources. The function
receives the choice as `API_TYPE`.{% if gateway %} The gateway forwards `GET`, `POST`, `PUT`,
`PATCH` and `DELETE` on every path to the function with the path unchanged.{% endif %}
{%- endif %}
{%- if gateway and timeout > 600 %}

The gateway waits for the function for at most 600 seconds, the maximum API
Gateway accepts. The function itself may run for ${{ timeout }} seconds, but a
caller gets a gateway timeout after 600.
{%- elif gateway %}

The gateway waits for the function as long as the function timeout
(`function_timeout_seconds`, at most 600 seconds at the gateway).
{%- endif %}
{%- if gateway and region == 'asia-east1' %}

API Gateway is not offered in `asia-east1`. The gateway{% if values.enableWaf %} and the load balancer's endpoint group{% endif %}
are created in `asia-northeast1` (`gateway_region`); everything else is in
`asia-east1`.
{%- endif %}
{%- if values.enableThrottling %}

### Throttling
{% if gatewayQuota %}
The API config defines a quota of `throttle_rate_limit` x 60 requests per
minute. API Gateway counts per minute and per consumer project, that is per
project that owns the calling API key: give each client a key from its own
project to limit clients separately.
{%- elif armorThrottle %}
API Gateway quotas need an API key to identify the caller, so with
authentication `${{ auth }}` the rate limit is a Cloud Armor throttle rule
instead: `throttle_rate_limit` x 60 requests per minute for each client IP
address (Cloud Armor accepts at most 1,000,000 per interval); excess requests
get status 429.{% if gateway %} Requests sent straight to the gateway address are not counted.{% endif %}
{%- else %}
API Gateway quotas need an API key to identify the caller, and no WAF was
selected that could count requests per client address. With authentication
`${{ auth }}` and no WAF the rate limit of ${{ values.throttleRateLimit or 1000 }} requests per second
is therefore **not enforced** and has no variable; only the burst limit below
applies. A rate limit needs API key authentication or the WAF.
{%- endif %}

Google Cloud has no token bucket with a burst size. `throttle_burst_limit` is
the number of requests the function serves at the same time: its instance
limit is `throttle_burst_limit / function_concurrency`, rounded up and capped
by `function_max_instances`. Requests beyond that wait briefly for a free
instance and then get status 429.
{%- endif %}
{%- if auth != 'none' %}

### Authentication
{% if auth == 'api-key' %}
Clients send an API key in the `x-api-key` header. Terraform creates one key,
restricted to this API; read it with `terraform output -raw api_key`. Create
further keys in **APIs & Services > Credentials** and restrict them to the
managed service shown by the `api_managed_service` output.
{%- elif auth == 'jwt' %}
Clients send a Firebase Authentication ID token as
`Authorization: Bearer <token>`. The gateway accepts tokens issued for the
Firebase project `firebase_project_id` (default: this project). Firebase
Authentication (Identity Platform) has to be set up in that project before
tokens can be issued; Terraform does not do that, because it is a project-wide
setting that cannot be removed again.
{%- elif auth == 'oauth2' %}
Clients send an access or ID token of your identity provider as
`Authorization: Bearer <token>`. The gateway checks signature, issuer and
audience against the required variables `oauth2_issuer`, `oauth2_jwks_uri` and
`oauth2_audiences`. They have no defaults: plan and apply fail until they are
set.
{%- else %}
Callers are Google service accounts. They send a Google-signed ID token as
`Authorization: Bearer <token>` whose audience is the `iam_audience` output:

```bash
gcloud auth print-identity-token \
  --impersonate-service-account=CALLER@PROJECT.iam.gserviceaccount.com \
  --audiences="$(terraform output -raw iam_audience)" --include-email
```

The gateway checks that Google signed the token for that audience. It cannot
check IAM roles, so every Google identity with such a token passes. List the
service accounts that may call the API in `iam_allowed_callers`: the function
receives them as `ALLOWED_CALLERS` and has to compare them with the `email`
claim (the placeholder does).
{%- endif %}

The gateway replaces the `Authorization` header with its own token for the
function. The verified claims of the caller arrive base64url encoded in
`X-Apigateway-Api-Userinfo`, the original header in
`X-Forwarded-Authorization`.
{%- endif %}
{%- if values.enableWaf %}

### WAF

The Cloud Armor policy blocks requests that match the preconfigured rule sets
for SQL injection, cross-site scripting, local and remote file inclusion and
remote code execution (sensitivity 1).

Without `load_balancer_domain` the load balancer has no certificate and serves
**plain HTTP** on its IP address (`load_balancer_ip` output). Use that for a
first test only. For real traffic set `load_balancer_domain`, apply, and point
the domain's DNS A record at `load_balancer_ip`: Terraform then creates a
Google-managed certificate and an HTTPS listener (TLS 1.2 or newer) and
redirects HTTP to HTTPS. The certificate becomes active after the DNS record
resolves, which can take up to an hour.
{%- endif %}
{%- if values.enableCache %}

### Cache

Redis is reachable only inside the VPC network. The function sends traffic for
private addresses through the connector and connects with TLS and AUTH:
`REDIS_HOST`, `REDIS_PORT`, `REDIS_CA_CERT` (certificate authority to trust)
and `REDIS_AUTH_STRING` (from Secret Manager). The connector runs two to three
`e2-micro` instances and is billed while it exists.
{%- endif %}

### Logs

The logs of the function{% if gateway %}, the gateway{% endif %}{% if values.enableWaf %} and the load balancer{% endif %} are stored in
the log bucket `${{ name }}-logs` for `log_retention_days` days. They are
excluded from the project's `_Default` bucket so that its retention does not
apply to them. The Logs Explorer shows them as before.

## Function package

The repository holds no application code. The function runs a zip archive of
**source code**; Cloud Build builds it when the function is created or the
archive changes. Supply the archive in one of two ways:

| Variable | Use |
| -------- | --- |
| `function_source_archive` | Path of a zip file on the machine that runs Terraform. Terraform uploads it to the source bucket |
| `function_source_bucket` and `function_source_object` | A zip file that is already in Cloud Storage, for example uploaded by the application's pipeline. The build service account (`${{ name }}` function build) needs read access to that bucket |

The archive contains the source at its top level, with the dependency file of
the language ({% if runtime == 'nodejs24' %}`package.json`{% elif runtime == 'python313' %}`requirements.txt` next to `main.py`{% elif runtime == 'go126' %}`go.mod`{% elif runtime == 'java21' %}`pom.xml` or `build.gradle`{% else %}a `.csproj` file{% endif %}). `function_entry_point` names
the function in it (default `${{ entryPoints[runtime] }}`).
{%- if interpreted %}

While none of these variables is set, Terraform packages the placeholder in
`placeholder/${{ 'nodejs' if runtime == 'nodejs24' else 'python' }}/` and deploys that, so the first apply works without a
build. The placeholder answers every request with a short JSON document and
shows how to read the environment variables and headers described here.
{%- else %}

`${{ runtime }}` has no placeholder in this repository. **Plan and apply fail with
"No function package" until one of the variables above is set.** Everything
else (`terraform validate`, the `CI` workflow) works without it.
{%- endif %}

