### Traffic shift
{% if strategy == 'all-at-once' %}
A new function revision takes all traffic as soon as it is ready
(`all_traffic_on_latest_revision = true`).
{%- else %}
A 2nd gen function is a Cloud Run service, and Cloud Run splits traffic
between revisions. Terraform cannot express a staged shift for a function: it
only sets `all_traffic_on_latest_revision = false`, which stops a deployment
from overriding the traffic split of the service. The shift itself is done
with `gcloud`
{%- if values.enableCicd %} by the `apply` job of the Deploy workflow:

1. Before the apply it assigns all traffic to the serving revision by name, so
   the new revision starts with no traffic.
2. After the apply it gives the new revision ${{ '10%' if strategy == 'canary' else '25%, then 50%, then 75%' }} and waits
   `ROLLOUT_STEP_SECONDS` (default 300)${{ ' after each step' if strategy == 'linear' else '' }}.
3. It sends all traffic to the latest revision.

Cancel the run during a wait to stop the rollout, then roll back:

```bash
gcloud run services update-traffic ${{ name }} --region ${{ region }} \
  --to-revisions <previous revision>=100
```

The workflow does not look at error rates; watch the{% if values.enableAlarms %} alert policies{% else %} function's metrics{% endif %}
during the wait. A `terraform apply` outside the workflow does not shift in
steps: the new revision takes whatever share the service gives to its latest
revision, which is all traffic after a completed rollout. After a rollout
`terraform plan` may report `all_traffic_on_latest_revision` changing from
`true` to `false`; that is the same setting being applied again.
{%- else %}. No deployment workflow was requested, so run the steps yourself:

```bash
# Before terraform apply: keep traffic on the serving revision
gcloud run services update-traffic ${{ name }} --region ${{ region }} \
  --to-revisions <serving revision>=100
terraform apply
# After it: ${{ 'give the new revision 10%, check, then everything' if strategy == 'canary' else 'raise the share of the new revision in steps (25, 50, 75), then everything' }}
gcloud run services update-traffic ${{ name }} --region ${{ region }} \
  --to-revisions LATEST=${{ '10' if strategy == 'canary' else '25' }}
gcloud run services update-traffic ${{ name }} --region ${{ region }} --to-latest
```

Without the first command the new revision takes all traffic at once, because
a service that follows its latest revision switches as soon as that revision
is ready.
{%- endif %}
{%- endif %}
